# Rug plots: geom_rug

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_rug()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_rug.html), in the order the page
gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot
draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of
what remains.

A rug plot adds the two one-dimensional distributions to a two-dimensional display, such as a
scatter plot: a short line at the edge of the panel for each row's `x`, and another for its `y`. It
draws every row, so it suits small tables. Giggly has no rugs yet, but `Giggly.Plot.geom_point/2`
draws the scatter plot they are added to. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells
write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The cells draw `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor
Trend* US magazine, which the setup cell binds to `mtcars`, R's name for it.

## A scatter plot

```r
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point()
p
```

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> render.()
```

## Rugs along the sides

```r
p + geom_rug()
p + geom_rug(sides="b")    # Rug on bottom only
p + geom_rug(sides="trbl") # All four sides
```

By default the rug runs along the bottom and the left, `sides = "bl"`.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_rug()` and its `sides` option.

## Jittering the rug

```r
# Use jittering to avoid overplotting for smaller datasets
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, cty)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_rug()

ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, cty)) +
  geom_jitter() +
  geom_rug(alpha = 1/2, position = "jitter")
```

`displ` and `cty` take few distinct values, so many cars share a point and a rug line. Jittering
moves each by a small random amount, so they can be told apart.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_rug()`, `geom_jitter()`, a fixed `alpha`, and `position_jitter()`.

## Rugs outside the panel

```r
# move the rug tassels to outside the plot
# remember to set clip = "off".
p +
  geom_rug(outside = TRUE) +
  coord_cartesian(clip = "off")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_rug()` and its `outside` option, and `coord_cartesian()`'s `clip`
option.

## Rugs on the top and right

```r
# set sides to top right, and then move the margins
p +
  geom_rug(outside = TRUE, sides = "tr") +
  coord_cartesian(clip = "off") +
  theme(plot.margin = margin_auto(1, unit = "cm"))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_rug()` and its `outside` and `sides` options, `coord_cartesian()`'s
`clip` option, and changing the theme, here to give the plot a margin of 1 cm on every side.

## Longer rug lines

```r
# increase the line length and
# expand axis to avoid overplotting
p +
  geom_rug(length = unit(0.05, "npc")) +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = c(0.1, 0.1))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_rug()` and its `length` option, and `scale_y_continuous()` and its
`expand` option.
